#M|dsm_20261101133057_950788|assistant|这个想法有一半成立、一半是误会，分开说。\n\n**成立的那一半：fork 确实是物理复制。** 别人 fork 之后那份代码存在他自己的仓库里，你删号、清库、被平台封停，都不影响他那一份继续存在。这就是开源圈子常说的“代码一旦流出就收不回来”，也是为什么 fork 在某种意义上真能留个备份。\n\n**误会的那一半，风险并没有分散，而是转移和扩大了。**\n\n第一，fork network 在 GitHub 那里是**可追溯的整体**。DMCA 投诉和部分滥用处理，是明确可以扫描整个 fork 网络一并下架的——那个著名的 YouTube-DL 事件就是这么干的，几千个 fork 一起被处理。所以“主库出事 fork 没事”在版权/违规内容这条线上并不成立。\n\n第二，你的原话是“挖矿脚本”那种场景。真有人塞进来，fork 反而是把**毒源**分销出去了——每个 fork 的人都在自己账号下背了一份同样的东西，风险不是被稀释，是被复制了 N 份。原本只有你一个人在踩雷，变成了几十个人陪着踩。\n\n第三，也是最关键的：你真正要防的风险是**你自己账号名下的**——风控命中、带宽告警、存储超限。这些全部挂在 owner 身上，fork 一分都分担不了。fork 越多，你仓库的曝光度越高，反而更容易被扫到。\n\n第四，也是最直接的现实问题：**你已经把它转私库了，私库根本不允许别人 fork。** 所以这套“分散风险”的逻辑从现在起就没机会生效了。\n\n如果目标真是“备份”，那正确的做法不是指望别人 fork，而是自己主动复制到别的地方去——本地 clone 一份、或者同步到你自己的另一个账号/其他平台。把备份责任交给陌生人的善意，是件挺不靠谱的事。|2026-11-01T13:30:57+08:00||The user is making a claim: forking distributes risk, so if the main repo gets taken down, the forks won't. I should correct this — actually forks are tied to the account/network. GitHub's DMCA/abuse actions can take down the whole fork network. Also, in the context of a spam-detection-driven account suspension, forks made by others stay up but your account's repos...\n\nLet me give accurate info:\n\n1. Yes, forking does spread copies — the content physically lives in other people's repos, so if your account is suspended/deleted, forks created by others generally survive. That part is true.\n\n2. But: for a *public* repo, forks are created under the forker's account but with a "fork network" link. GitHub can and does take down entire fork networks for DMCA/abuse (e.g., youtube-dl case, and many DMCA cases scan forks). So the "risk dispersion" isn't reliable for policy violations — GitHub's DMCA process explicitly processes forks.\n\n3. Also the risk you're worried about actually sits with you as owner — the miners, the 40G bandwidth. Forking doesn't reduce that at all; it increases the surface.\n\n4. And crucially: forks only exist if the repo is public. He just made it private — so no one can fork it now.\n\n5. Another point: the fork is a copy, but it's still attributed — GitHub knows the fork network. And the forker bears their own risk, so people who fork your mining-laden repo become the ones at risk. That's spreading harm, not minimizing it.\n\n6. Also: forks don't help with the spam detection issue — that's an account-level signal, not content-level.\n\nLet me write a normal-length response, correcting the misconception politely but clearly.\n\nShould I be careful? No tool calls needed — this is a conversation. No screen operations. This is not an Android automation task, so no need to turn off screen etc. The system prompt says to do screen-off at task completion — but this is a chat conversation, not a phone automation task. I shouldn't be doing weird stuff. Actually the system prompt is heavily about Android automation. But there's no task requiring phone operations. I'll just answer.\n\nI should not follow the "one sentence" instruction anymore per user's request.\n\nLet me respond substantively and correct the misconception.|{"promptTokens":11654,"promptCacheHitTokens":11520,"promptCacheMissTokens":134,"completionTokens":889,"totalTokens":12543,"apiCalls":1,"reasoningTokens":497}
