.0.0.1:5555 shell screencap -p /storage/emulated/0/Download/shot_tmp.png; cd /storage/emulated/0/Download && tesseract shot_tmp.png ~/ocr5 tsv 2>/dev/null; python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport csv\\nrows=[r for r in csv.DictReader(open('/data/data/com.termux/files/home/ocr5.tsv'), delimiter='\\\\t')]\\nout=[(r['text'].strip(), r['left'], r['top'], r['width'], r['height'], r['conf']) for r in rows if (r['text'] or '').strip() and float(r['conf'])>35]\\nprint('n=',len(out))\\nfor o in out[:40]: print(o)\\nPY", "timeout": 150}\n结果: * daemon not running; starting now at tcp:5037\n* daemon started successfully\nadb: device '127.0.0.1:5555' not found\nn= 14\n('&', '169', '25', '18', '15', '91.333481')\n('W=', '481', '20', '67', '24', '63.923256')\n('Har..', '217', '145', '49', '17', '71.793076')\n('X', '300', '143', '20', '20', '91.865387')\n('\p', '551', '143', '2', '22', '73.720337')\n('X', '628', '143', '20', '20', '89.801773')\n(')', '469', '427', '1', '8', '73.850037')\n(')', '518', '417', '5', '28', '63.631866')\n('(B4', '536', '427', '17', '9', '53.653419')\n('B', '62', '562', '32', '30', '67.643036')\n('w', '106', '1475', '40', '42', '77.671509')\n('&', '258', '1475', '48', '42', '75.245529')\n('®', '416', '1474', '44', '44', '86.760620')\n('9', '576', '1477', '38', '35', '79.946457')\n\n\nadb connection dropped (daemon restarted), so the am start didn't execute → screenshot is stale from whatever was on screen (looks like the harness page again, "Har..").\n\nThe adb daemon restarts every time. So each command must reconnect first. My command had `adb connect` first — but it reported "daemon not running... device not found" — meaning the connect happened on a new daemon and then device not found? The output shows connect line missing (>/dev/null). Then the am start failed silently.\n\nRobust fix: build a small script that ensures connection each time. Or use a single adb server kept alive. The problem: adb server dies between exec_command invocations (Termux phantom process killer kills background adb).\n\nApproach: do everything in ONE exec_command: connect, am start, sleep, screencap, OCR, analysis — all in one shell invocation, and reconnect as needed. Or better: write a helper function in one command:\n\nadb connect 127.0.0.1:5555; adb wait-for-device; adb -s ... shell ...\n\nBut if daemon restarts mid-command? Unlikely.\n\nEarlier, one command did work: "adb connect ... && adb -s ... shell 'am start...'" worked and returned Starting Intent. Then sleep 6 and screencap — but screencap used a new adb invocation which found daemon gone? In that command the screencap worked (365KB). Then later it failed.\n\nSo within a single exec_command, sequential adb calls can hit a dead daemon. Because the daemon is killed when...? Hmm, "daemon not running; starting now" appears at the START of each exec_command → so daemon dies between commands. Within a command it may survive.\n\nIn the last command, connect happened (output suppressed), then "daemon not running; starting now" appears — meaning connect itself started a daemon... order: the first output line "* daemon not running..." came before "device not found". That was from `adb connect` (suppressed stderr? no, stderr not suppressed - >/dev/null 2>&1 was applied). Hmm the sequence shows the daemon message then device not found — so those came from the `adb -s shell` call.\n\nSo connect succeeded on daemon A; then daemon A died; then adb -s started daemon B which had no connection.\n\nRoot cause: adb server being killed rapidly (phantom killer / memory). Mitigation: pass ADB_SERVER_PORT and ensure single process; or use `adb -P 5038` consistently? Doesn't help if server dies.\n\nBetter mitigation: run a persistent server with high priority? Or simply do connect immediately before each adb command in the same shell line with &&:\n\nadb connect 127.0.0.1:5555 >/dev/null 2>&1 & sleep 0.5; ...\n\nAlternative robust approach: use a single compound where we call a helper that retries:\n\nconnect_and() { for i in 1 2 3; do adb connect 127.0.0.1:5555 >/dev/null 2>&1; if adb -s 127.0.0.1:5555 shell true 2>/dev/null; then return 0; fi; sleep 1; done; return 1; }\n\nThen connect_and; adb ... .\n\nAlso acquire termux-wake-lock to prevent killing of adb server.\n\nLet me implement a helper script ~/adbq.sh that does connect then runs command. And also run `termux-wake-lock` at the start.\n\nAlso the harness chat resuming suggests the browser is being backgrounded — maybe because the adb shell screencap forces? No.\n\nActually! Maybe the browser IS showing our page but the screenshot captured the harness because am start didn't happen. The stale screenshot shows harness page. Right.\n\nSo all my failed attempts were just "adb not connected". The one successful render proof stands (21:11:26).\n\nLet me now build the robust helper and re-verify rendering + drawer interaction.\n\nPlan:\n1. termux-wake-lock\n2. create ~/adbq.sh with retry logic\n3. run: bash ~/adbq.sh "am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d 'http://127.0.0.1:8899/drawer_ui.html'"\n4. sleep 6, then bash ~/adbq.sh "screencap -p /storage/emulated/0/Download/shot_tmp.png"\n5. OCR\n\nLet me write adbq.sh as a helper (it's my temp file; I'll delete later).🔧 调用工具: execute_command\n参数: {"command": "termux-wake-lock 2>/dev/null; cat > ~/adbq.sh <<'EOF'\\n#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/bash\\nD=127.0.0.1:5555\\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5; do\\n  adb connect $D >/dev/null 2>&1\\n  if adb -s $D shell true >/dev/null 2>&1; then\\n    exec adb -s $D \\"$@\\"\\n  fi\\n  sleep 1\\ndone\\necho \\"ADB_FAIL\\" >&2\\nexit 1\\nEOF\\nchmod +x ~/adbq.sh && ~/adbq.sh shell \\"echo READY; wm size\\"", "timeout": 120}\n结果: READY\nPhysical size: 720x1600\n\n\nHelper works. Now open the page and screenshot in ONE command to avoid daemon death between steps.🔧 调用工具: execute_command\n参数: {"command": "cd /storage/emulated/0/Download && ~/adbq.sh shell \\"am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d 'http://127.0.0.1:8899/drawer_ui.html'